I just did an upgrade yesterday and Konqueror is SIGFPEing.  Scanned the=20
libraries for floating point use without trap statements, and there are bun=
ch=20
of them again.  Specifically (for Konqueror):

kdelibs4c2:  /usr/lib/libkutils.so.1
kdelibs4c2:  /usr/lib/libkio.so.4
kdelibs4c2:  /usr/lib/libkdesu.so.4
kdelibs4c2:  /usr/lib/libkdecore.so.4
kdelibs4c2:  /usr/lib/libkdefx.so.4
libqt3-mt:  /usr/lib/libqt-mt.so.3
libx11-6:  /usr/X11R6/lib/libX11.so.6
libaudio2:  /usr/lib/libaudio.so.2

I checked GCC4 (the current 4.0.2-2 release in unstable) and the '-mieee' i=
s=20
default unless '-ffinte-math-only' (turned on by '-ffast-math') stuff is=20
still all there and working.  I scanned the source packages and couldn't fi=
nd=20
any use of '-ffast-math' (other than for x86).

Anyone know what is going on here?  Is the GCC4 on the build machine differ=
ent=20
somehow?  (if someone has access they can check with my attached script by=
=20
just compiling up a small program that does some floating point math)

Thanks!  -T

PS:  Attached is a shell script that scans the programs given on the comman=
d=20
line (i.e., './check.sh konqeuror') and outputs for each program and its=20
libraries 'OKAY' if the math is all integer or trapped and 'FAIL' otherwise.

=2D-=20
 Tyson Whitehead  (-twhitehe@uwo.ca -- WSC-)
 Computer Engineer                          Dept. of Applied Mathematics,
 Graduate Student- Applied Mathematics      University of Western Ontario,
 GnuPG Key ID# 0x8A2AB5D8                   London, Ontario, Canada

--Boundary-01=_18sRDYV8IrsHirk
Content-Type: application/x-shellscript;
  name="check.sh"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
Content-Disposition: attachment;
	filename="check.sh"

#!/bin/bash

function scan {
  for file in "$@"; do
    echo -n "  $file: "
    if objdump -d "$file" | egrep -q '(add|sub|div|mul|cmp).*\$f'; then
      if objdump -d "$file" | egrep -q 'trap'; then
        echo "OKAY (trapped)"
      else
        echo "FAIL"
      fi
    else
      echo "OKAY (integer)"
    fi
  done
}

function libs {
  ldd "$1" | sed -e 's#^[ \t]*\(.*=>\)\{0,1\}[ \t]*\(.*[^ \t]\)[ \t]*(.*)[ \t]*$#\2#'
}

for file in "$@"; do
  echo "$file:"
  if [[ -r "$file" ]] || file=`which "$file"`; then
    scan `which "$file"` `libs "$file"`
  else
    echo "  NOT FOUND" 
  fi
done

